WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.303
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.438
writer, actor, and director who
you know from his work on

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.106
"Saturday Night Live,"
"Documentary Now!",

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:08.908
and HBO's "Barry."

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:09.943
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:11.244
our very good friend Bill Hader.

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:14.680
Hi, buddy.
-Hey, buddy. How's it going?

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.249
-Good. You know,
I think of you every day.

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:18.217
Viewers of our show will know
I have, of course,

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:20.386
my little Stefon nesting doll.

00:00:20.386 --> 00:00:22.989
Did you know you've been joined
by Andy Samberg

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:25.291
as a mummy whose doink fell off?

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:28.928
[ Both laugh ]

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:30.396
-My doink fell off?

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:33.633
-Yeah, Samberg played a mummy
whose doink fell off,

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.369
and at the end of it --
at the end of it,

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:37.903
I told him, "That went great,"

00:00:37.903 --> 00:00:44.310
and he said, "Oh, do I get to be
on the desk then?"

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.580
-He's always gunning
for my spot, man.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.215
-Yeah, he wants your spot.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.884
I think that you probably get
asked this all the time --

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:55.555
I know I do -- how do you think
Stefon would be doing

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:56.956
during a pandemic?

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:58.924
It strikes me that a lot of
what he does

00:00:58.924 --> 00:01:01.160
would probably be
shut down right now.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:02.929
-I don't know
if he would notice.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:05.564
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:08.768
I think he would kind of go for
a while and not really notice

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:09.936
and then --

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:10.937
-That's true.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:12.304
And I guess this -- He does --

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:14.106
This is like
a natural mask, right?

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:17.476
-Yeah, it was a mask, yeah.
It was kind of a mask.

00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:21.948
And he would -- I think he was
probably, you know,

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:23.750
patient zero on some level.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:26.919
And he's probably had it a
couple times, and now he's fine.

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:29.088
-Yeah. I mean,
it does seem like --

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:32.091
Yeah, he probably went to
a club at some point

00:01:32.091 --> 00:01:33.559
that was called Wet Market.

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:35.628
[ Both laugh ]

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:36.796
-Yeah.

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:38.865
-I do feel like a lot of
the clubs that he went to

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:41.234
in New York City might not be
following, like,

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.236
de Blasio's rules.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:45.871
Like, it feels like they were
already pretty underground.

00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:47.040
-They were all
pretty underground,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.642
if they, in fact, exist,
you know?

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:52.378
-Right. Are you saying,
this whole time,

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:54.513
Stefon might have been
projecting a version of himself

00:01:54.513 --> 00:01:55.848
that wasn't entirely true?

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:58.484
-That's a thing I always laughed
about, because, you know,

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:00.586
when I would laugh doing that,

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:04.290
so much of it was just how bad
at his job he is,

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.692
you know, that he's lying.

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:10.162
And I always felt that
you were very --

00:02:10.162 --> 00:02:11.430
You knew he was lying,

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.932
but you were being
very patient with him.

00:02:13.932 --> 00:02:16.669
-Well, I remember Neal Brennan,
our friend Neal Brennan,

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:20.340
pointing out that he liked to
think of the producer backstage

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.341
with Stefon right before he went
on "Weekend Update"

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:24.110
being like, "Okay,
and just a reminder, Stefon.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.779
We really want
St. Patrick's Day tips,

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:28.581
if you could just keep it
themed to that,"

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:31.350
and Stefon just, like,
promising this would be the time

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.085
that he would actually --

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:34.654
-Not listening to him at all.

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:35.922
He thinks -- He's like,

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:40.159
"The giant frog with the
headphone backstage told me.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:43.963
-Did you -- I know you had
to put a pause on

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:48.167
Season 3 of "Barry,"
which I'm heartbroken by

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.936
because I'm
so excited to see it.

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:52.471
Where are you in production now?

00:02:52.471 --> 00:02:56.976
-We were in -- Like right when
the lockdown happened in March,

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.779
we were a week away
from shooting.

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:03.715
So the last time I was in
a room with a lot of people

00:03:03.715 --> 00:03:07.119
was the table read for
episodes 1 and 2 of Season 3,

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:08.254
and we were ready to go.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:11.056
And then it all got shut down.

00:03:11.056 --> 00:03:13.125
So, we actually wrote --

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:15.528
Season 3 is completely written,
ready to go.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.063
And then, during this time,

00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:20.399
we actually went
and wrote Season 4.

00:03:20.399 --> 00:03:23.368
So we have both of those
kind of done.

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:26.972
So we're just waiting until we
can start shooting again.

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:28.508
-That's really exciting.

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:34.179
I imagine -- I mean, there was
such a wonderful cliffhanger

00:03:34.179 --> 00:03:37.049
at the end of Season 2, but did
you worry that you had,

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:38.918
to some degree,
written yourself into a corner

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:41.086
that you had to find
a way out of?

00:03:41.086 --> 00:03:45.991
-That's kind of what we do at
the end of every season.

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:47.160
You know,
at the end of Season 1,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.062
we had written ourselves
this kind of cliffhanger,

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.797
and then -- Yeah,
and then you do it again.

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:53.666
And then, you know,
Alec Berg and I,

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:54.767
who I created the show with --

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.435
We were just talking about
this last night,

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:59.304
where it's a bit like having
a reoccurring character

00:03:59.304 --> 00:04:00.373
on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:04:00.373 --> 00:04:02.442
like we were just
talking about Stefon.

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:03.942
You kind of do
the first time you go out --

00:04:03.942 --> 00:04:05.778
it's like the first season,
where you're like,

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:06.846
"Oh, that was fun.

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:08.681
It seems like they liked it,"
you know?

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:10.482
And then, the second time,
you have a little pressure.

00:04:10.482 --> 00:04:11.784
You get recognition.

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:14.654
And then, by third time
you're doing it, you're --

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:17.323
The more I did Stefon or
the more we did Californians

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.891
or whatever, you always --

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:21.393
You know, it got just more
tense, you know?

00:04:21.393 --> 00:04:25.331
More pressure on you to, like,
do new moves or whatever.

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:28.834
And on "Barry,"
I can't just rely on

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:30.435
John Mulaney to make me laugh.

00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:34.673
So -- my lines, you know?

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:41.747
So, yeah, you know, it was --
It is very, very hard.

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:44.116
And, yeah, we ended on a
cliffhanger in the last year.

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:46.052
And, yeah, first day back in
the writers' room,

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:49.288
it was like, you know, everyone
going, "So, what happens?"

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:51.957
And we were like,
"I have no idea.

00:04:51.957 --> 00:04:54.093
Really, really screwed
ourselves here."

00:04:54.093 --> 00:04:55.694
And we're still saying it.

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:58.397
Like, yeah, we're still trying
to figure it out.

00:04:58.397 --> 00:04:59.465
-I can't wait.

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.267
Obviously, I'm very eager
for you guys

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.635
to get back to work on that.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:03.936
And I have more to
talk to you about.

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:05.705
We'll be right back
with more Bill Hader.

